    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • Denza Launching \"God's Eye\" Driving Assistance in Europe, the Z9GT Will Get It First\nAudi's Hottest Electric Wagon Yet Shows Wider Arches, Might Not Be Called RS6 Avant e-tron\n14 photos\nPhoto: SH Proshots\nIngolstadt is currently putting the finishing touches on a high-performance longroof with all-electric propulsion instead of a hybridized V8. Originally believed to be christened RS 6 Avant e-tron, the newcomer may be called something different.\nPer insider sources cited by Top Gear back in September 2025, the RS 6 e-tron project was canceled, leaving the S6 e-tron as the flagship. Audi reportedly decided on this due to dismally low projected sales. Furthermore, the RS treatment would also jeopardize the already struggling RS e-tron GT.\nHaving cleared that up, hearsay suggests S6 e-tron performance as the name of this version. To be offered in both sedan and wagon flavors, the all-wheel-drive bruiser definitely looks more purposeful compared to the non-performance S6 e-tron. Spied near the most demanding circuit in the world, the Avant-bodied prototype sits lower and is definitely wider where it actually matters.\nThe heavily flared wheel arches are complemented by vertical openings beside the front doors, namely vents that improve airflow along the bodysides while also reducing front-end aerodynamic lift by relieving the high-pressure air that builds up inside the wheel wells. Trapped air creates high-pressure zones that act against the underside of the fenders, pushing the front of the car upward.\nIts face carries the split-headlight setup and covered Singleframe grille of the S6 e-tron, yet the front bumper makes it a Performance model. Redesigned intakes are on the menu, along with a more prominent splitter designed to better manage airflow at high speeds and under spirited cornering.\nPhoto: SH Proshots Moving along its flanks, large alloys with a diamond-cut finish and six double spokes appear to be intended for series production. The side skirts nicely tie the more aggressive front end into the backside, giving the S6 e-tron Performance a more planted silhouette. At the rear, changes include a slightly different roof spoiler and a larger aerodynamic diffuser that looks better integrated into the bumper than the diffuser of the S6 e-tron.\nLook closer, and you will further notice upgraded brakes that definitely make sense on a car that carries a bigger punch and more purposeful aero. Regarding that bigger punch, it's worth remembering that Audi lists the S6 e-tron with 543 horsepower in launch mode and no more than 631 pound-feet (855 Newton-meters) of twist from dual electric drive units.\nThe S6 Avant e-tron requires 3.9 seconds to hit 100 kilometers per hour, onto a top speed of 240 kilometers per hour. In American speak, those would be 3.7 seconds to 60 miles per hour and 149 miles an hour on the right tires. Because of the wider body and downforce-favoring updates, the S6 e-tron Performance may not match the maximum speed of the S6 e-tron.\nBeyond the aforementioned goodies, the Performance version may also come with a sportier interior. More bolstering for the front seats and carbon-fiber trim would be great differentiators that would cost Audi little in the way of R&D money. Considering that S6 e-tron Performance designates a step below the stillborn RS 6 e-tron, one has to wonder how many of these will actually be sold in the first year of production.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nAudi S6 e-tron performance 2028 Audi S6 e-tron Performance Audi 2028 Audi RS 6 e-tron Performance spy shots Audi RS 6 e-tron Performance spyshots\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Bell MV-75 Cheyenne II Black Hawk Replacement Moves Closer to First Test Flight",
